    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Pixel-identical to the original. Clone is done and written to the requested path.\n\nSummary:\n\n- **Investigation first**: crow-nester's outline showed a simple `#main` list with an `h1` and three uniform `.p-4` blocks (each an avatar image + quote). I flagged those three as the priority group up front since they dominate the page.\n- **Key discovery**: the raw HTML carries Tailwind utility classes (`rounded-lg`, `shadow-md`, `flex`, etc.) on every element, which looks like a strong signal to wire up a Tailwind CDN build. But the source page has **no Tailwind runtime, no `<style>`, no CSS at all** — those class names are inert, and a real-browser screenshot of the original confirms it renders as completely unstyled default HTML (default h1, plain stacked paragraphs, broken `<img>` icons with alt text showing, since `/avatar-*.jpg` don't resolve locally). Following the letter of \"convert Tailwind classes to a config\" here would have actively hurt fidelity — it would have rendered rounded shadowed cards that don't match the real page at all. So I deliberately did *not* add a Tailwind CDN script.\n- **Componentization**: red-twine confirmed the three `div(img,p)` blocks share identical shape, and reading their content confirmed they're genuinely the same kind of thing (testimonial: avatar + quote), so I treated them as one repeated component rather than coincidence.\n- **Unrecreatable/flagged**: the avatar images (`/avatar-1.jpg`, `-2.jpg`, `-3.jpg`) are broken in the original too (relative paths with no server backing them in this fixture) — I preserved the exact same `src`/`alt` attributes rather than inventing a placeholder image, per the hotlink-don't-rehost instruction, so the clone reproduces the same broken-image presentation as the source.\n- **Fidelity check**: screenshotted both original and clone at the same 1280×900 viewport and ran odiff — result was **0% pixel difference, exit code 0 (exact match)**. No further iteration rounds were needed since the first pass was already pixel-perfect; screenshots for both rounds (`original.png`, `clone-round1.png`, `diff-round1.png`) are saved in the run directory."
    }
